WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.970
Good morning Hank; it's Monday, July 4, 2011.

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:05.380
It's Independence Day here in the United States,
and today I'm going to talk about financial

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:09.370
independence, specifically how young people
can turn their crappy summer jobs into hundreds

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:14.030
of thousands of dollars... or the equivalent
in euro, pounds, rial, yen, whatever.

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:16.710
By the way, Hank, I'm dressed up and giving
financial advice because I was in The Wall

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:21.970
Street Journal last week, which means that
I am now technically an expert in money.

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:25.369
Okay, Hank, so when I was in high school,
I worked at a warehouse stacking stereo equipment

00:00:25.369 --> 00:00:29.039
of various shapes and sizes into semi trucks.
And the game was that you had to fill the

00:00:29.039 --> 00:00:33.140
semi trucks as much as possible in order to
minimize the number of semi trucks that it

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:37.531
took to move the stereo equipment around.
It was basically like manual labor Tetris.

00:00:37.531 --> 00:00:41.520
Really, it was more like manual labor Tetris
in an un-air-conditioned, 110 degree warehouse

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.170
with a lot of people yelling at you about
how much you suck at Tetris. Also, I made

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:48.280
$7.50 an hour, which is by no stretch of the
imagination a living wage, unless you have

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.520
someone paying for your food, clothing, and
housing.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:54.450
Okay, so let's say you make $7.50 an hour
at your crappy summer job. That's $247 dollars

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:57.469
a week in take-home pay. You probably work
about 10 weeks over the summer; you would

00:00:57.469 --> 00:01:01.309
work 12, but your parents are making you go
on a crappy summer vacation to like, Mount

00:01:01.309 --> 00:01:04.640
Rushmore or something, and they're gonna fight
the entire time about your dad's driving,

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.361
and there won't be 3G internet half the time,
and you won't be able to talk to your friends,

00:01:07.361 --> 00:01:09.900
and it's gonna suuuuck!

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.229
On the other hand, they did pay for your diapers
all those years ago.

00:01:12.229 --> 00:01:17.260
So, $247 a week over 10 weeks. I'm not a mathematician,
Hank, but I think that's $2,470 over the course

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.783
of the summer. 'Course, you're going to have
to spend some of that money - you gotta put

00:01:19.783 --> 00:01:22.079
aside some money for Hank's new album, Ellen
Hardcastle, you're gonna have to see Deathly

00:01:22.079 --> 00:01:25.049
Hallows, you're probably gonna go out to dinner
with your friends a few times... Let's say,

00:01:25.049 --> 00:01:26.189
you know, 500 bucks.

00:01:26.189 --> 00:01:29.619
Okay, so that leaves you with $1,970. This
is the part where I want you to go to the

00:01:29.619 --> 00:01:34.400
bank and set up an IRA, which is not an Irish
Republican Army, but an Individual Retirement

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.860
Account. If you don't live in the US, they
call it something different, but there are

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:39.260
equivalents from Brazil to Europe, pretty
much everywhere.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.930
Hank, I'm a very fancy and highly-trained
economist, so I'm not sure this explanation

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:46.240
is going to make sense to you, but an IRA
is essentially a machine that produces money

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:50.259
using nothing but time. How does it do it?
Through the magic of compound interest. So

00:01:50.259 --> 00:01:53.840
let's say you have a $10 investment that you're
somehow making 10% on. At the end of the year,

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.659
you've made a dollar in interest, and you
have $11. At the end of the next year, you

00:01:57.659 --> 00:02:02.539
get 10% of that $11, which is $1.10, and your
interest just goes up and up and up and up.

00:02:02.539 --> 00:02:06.680
So let's say between the ages of 16 and 19
you're able to put away $1,970 a year into

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:10.850
an IRA account, and then you do nothing for
50 years. We know that the world economy grows,

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:14.630
when adjusted for inflation, about 6.5% a
year over the last 100 years. That means that

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:19.970
at the end of 50 years, when you retire at
the age of 69, your $5,910 will have turned

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:24.050
into more than $162,000, and yes I am adjusting
for inflation.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:27.940
$162,000 from stupid, getting yelled at, manual
labor Tetris!

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:31.020
But realistically, you probably can't put
away $2,000 a summer. I mean, you may have

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.890
to save for college or you may have to help
out your family. You may have to pre-order

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:37.860
26 copies of The Fault in Our Stars. So let's
say between the ages of 16 and 21 you put

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:44.070
away $400 a summer, for a total of $2,000.
You still have more than $50,000 when you

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:46.900
retire. That means when it's all said and
done you didn't make $7.50 an hour flipping

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:48.670
burgers, you made $125 an hour.

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:54.201
But now let's say I, toddering old man that
I am at 33 years old, put away $2,000 today.

00:02:54.201 --> 00:02:59.150
I'm still 37 years away from retirement, but
what happens if I put away $2,000? It's only

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:03.280
worth $20,000. That's compound interest at
work. That's why it's more important to save

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.540
early, to maximize the number of years that
the interest can compound, than it is to save

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:08.540
lots.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:10.930
At a lot of banks, you can open up these accounts
for, like, $100. And the bankers are always

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:14.250
happy to help you because they get paid for
each account they open. So there really is

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:17.750
a way to turn a crappy summer job into a lot
of money, although it's going to take a while.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:21.670
In short, Hank: you don't have to be a rich
person to do the things that rich people do

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:23.920
to maximize their income and avoid taxes.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.680
Alright Hank, before I answer the pressing
question of whether my suit is all business,

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:29.860
there is one outstanding issue: YouTube NextUp
Europe.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:33.340
There are a lot of nerdfighters who made it
to the voting round of YouTube NextUp Europe,

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:37.370
including Rosianna, who is responsible for
the title of The Fault in Our Stars. I've

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:40.590
put links to entries from known nerdfighters
in the doobly-doo. It's really easy - you

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:44.120
just click thumbs-up. But the most important
thing is that you watch all the videos and

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:48.260
vote for your favorites because you have a
chance to shape the future of YouTube, and

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:51.870
that's something you should take seriously.
I hope that you do. I watched every single

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:53.850
video and I hope you will, too.

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:56.100
I made a poem.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:57.610
It's not. I'll see you on Wednesday.

